{"lines":[{"h":0.008720930389296444,"x":0.7716049391467531,"t":"1718867188","y":0.046511627815394085,"w":0.069958845774332645,"c":0.30000001192092896},{"c":1,"h":0.013081395583944611,"x":0.1172839502042468,"t":"Test 3","y":0.053779069625663367,"w":0.057613169352213542},{"c":1,"t":"The dramatic decline of the thylacine in Tasmania, which began in the 1830s and continued for","w":0.75720163981119792,"x":0.11728395366778917,"y":0.09738372077989399,"h":0.015988372380376759},{"y":0.11333564141618324,"w":0.75525212605794256,"h":0.016065789055346835,"x":0.11723959649794902,"c":1,"t":"a century, is generally attributed to the relentless efforts of sheep farmers and bounty hunters**"},{"w":0.76543207804361979,"h":0.01472320376914027,"y":0.13221436958360666,"c":1,"t":"with shotguns. While this determined campaign undoubtedly played a large part, it is likely that","x":0.11522634770600708},{"x":0.11522634677118239,"w":0.77160491943359377,"y":0.14811542992806159,"c":1,"t":"various other factors also contributed to the decline and eventual extinction of the species. These","h":0.014723203769140158},{"t":"include competition with wild dogs introduced by European settlers, loss of habitat along with","y":0.16424418589796042,"w":0.75308639526367183,"h":0.015988372380376759,"x":0.11522634959791409,"c":1},{"y":0.18168433446977206,"h":0.015990084276322869,"t":"the disappearance of prey species, and a distemper-like disease which may also have affected","w":0.74279836018880219,"x":0.11522633420687262,"c":1},{"h":0.016109428922475688,"t":"the thylacine.","w":0.11387022654215494,"c":1,"y":0.19666273596697015,"x":0.11492201791809549},{"x":0.11934155332104902,"y":0.23110465103010491,"t":"There was only one successful attempt to breed a thylacine in captivity, at Melbourne Zoo in","w":0.73456792195638021,"c":1,"h":0.014534883982160851},{"c":1,"x":0.11723795921121233,"t":"1899. This was despite the large numbers that went through some zoos, particularly London Zoo","h":0.017198193059231537,"w":0.7696378072102863,"y":0.24702809710466511},{"x":0.11522633326500455,"y":0.26442873986145066,"h":0.01472320376914027,"c":1,"w":0.74897120157877606,"t":"and Tasmania's Hobart Zoo. The famous naturalist John Gould foresaw the thylacine's demise"},{"y":0.28052325566799263,"x":0.11728396088177619,"w":0.76543207804361968,"t":"when he published his Mammals of Australia between 1848 and 1863, writingạiạỊạỊThe numbers of","c":1,"h":0.016002068109590928},{"y":0.29651162775219686,"h":0.01598837238037687,"x":0.11522633797543222,"t":"this singular animal will speedily diminish, extermination will have its full sway, and it will then,","c":1,"w":0.77572016398111976},{"x":0.11517685792566947,"y":0.31248002037632572,"c":1,"w":0.60918258666992187,"h":0.017851363362917816,"t":"like the wolf of England and Scotland, be recorded as an animal of the past?"},{"w":0.7654320780436199,"h":0.01453488398216074,"t":"However, there seems to have been little public pressure to preserve the thylacine, nor was much","x":0.11934157404375639,"y":0.34738372079765845,"c":1},{"t":"concern expressed by scientists at the decline of this species in the decades that followed. A","y":0.36336866910926768,"x":0.11728394107967972,"w":0.72839508056640623,"h":0.013084819375836831,"c":1},{"t":"notable exception was T.T. Flynn, Professor of Biology at the University of Tasmania. In 1914,","x":0.11728398045585607,"y":0.37926972920346391,"h":0.014723203769140047,"c":1,"w":0.75514399210611982},{"t":"he was sufficiently concerned about the scarcity of the thylacine to suggest that some should be","y":0.39510116915979387,"c":1,"w":0.7614112854003906,"x":0.11722909416695472,"h":0.016937010981870948},{"c":1,"t":"captured and placed on a small island. But it was not until 1929, with the species on the very edge","h":0.017441860778592888,"y":0.41279069751450903,"w":0.78395060221354163,"x":0.11522634486442257},{"t":"of extinction, that Tasmania's Animals and Birds Protection Board passed a motion protecting","c":1,"x":0.11522633323719124,"w":0.74897120157877606,"h":0.014555427856804748,"y":0.43021201413167087},{"w":0.77983540852864586,"c":1,"x":0.11522632854441459,"t":"thylacines only for the month of December, which was thought to be their prime breeding season.","y":0.44611307446370252,"h":0.016195524146054185},{"c":1,"h":0.014546867815428799,"x":0.11728394737328975,"y":0.46366279056501769,"t":"The last known wild thylacine to be killed was shot by a farmer in the north-east of Tasmania","w":0.74279836018880196},{"t":"in 1930, leaving just captive specimens. Official protection of the species by the Tasmanian","x":0.11522632792368266,"c":1,"y":0.47965116264469709,"w":0.72839508056640623,"h":0.015988372380376759},{"t":"government was introduced in July 1936, 59 days before the last known individual died in Hobart","y":0.49705535915208543,"h":0.016026036337772775,"c":1,"x":0.11522632857306414,"w":0.77983540852864597},{"y":0.51295641949237059,"c":1,"t":"Zoo on 7th September, 1936.","x":0.11728395286632311,"h":0.016195524146054185,"w":0.2345678965250651},{"c":1,"y":0.54505300371425436,"t":"There have been numerous expeditions and searches for the thylacine over the years, none of","x":0.1193415696561984,"h":0.01454001966999896,"w":0.73868311564127609},{"h":0.014723203769140158,"w":0.78395060221354163,"x":0.11728395804240904,"t":"which has produced definitive evidence that thylacines still exist. The species was declared extinct","y":0.56242638384842025,"c":1},{"t":"by the Tasmanian government in 1986.","y":0.57979976455493554,"w":0.31275721232096354,"c":1,"x":0.11728394517418622,"h":0.013250883392226132},{"x":0.11721016415304414,"y":0.8458924210028681,"c":1,"w":0.40547714233398441,"h":0.012990397193827774,"t":"** bounty hunters: people who are paid a reward for killing a wild animal"},{"y":0.86773255795657411,"x":0.11728395072044402,"h":0.013081395583944722,"c":1,"t":"60","w":0.024691357612609863},{"h":0.033792529426278195,"c":1,"t":"IELTSXpress.com","y":0.95311055968589831,"x":0.66991094639740412,"w":0.30613845825195307}],"h":3395,"w":2400}